00:04:12.792 --> 00:04:17.972
and related hardware changes the cost curve for every company in that chain. Tesla included.

00:04:18.412 --> 00:04:23.992
Tesla continues to ship megapacks and power walls at growing volumes, while new competitors move

00:04:23.992 --> 00:04:29.712
into the same supply chains for cells, inverters, and transformers. Capital flowing into projects

00:04:29.712 --> 00:04:35.512
speeds learning on installation times, permitting, and grid interconnection, which lowers the soft

00:04:35.512 --> 00:04:41.392
costs that still dominate many deployments. Tesla's own factories benefit when suppliers see larger

00:04:41.392 --> 00:04:44.692
order books and can invest in higher yields or new chemistries.

00:04:45.312 --> 00:04:50.912
The same surge raises the bar for differentiation, a company that once stood out mainly by offering

00:04:50.912 --> 00:04:55.872
a complete hardware plus software package now competes against well-funded specialists

00:04:55.872 --> 00:04:58.712
focused only on the battery or only on the inverter.

00:04:59.352 --> 00:05:03.732
Over the longer term, the funding wave tests whether vertical integration still delivers

00:05:03.732 --> 00:05:06.072
an edge once capital is abundant.

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:14.612
Tesla has long argued that owning cell production, power electronics and fleet software together reduces friction and improves margins.

00:05:15.132 --> 00:05:24.792
If that growth in storage investment sustains, the test is whether those integrated margins hold or whether pure-place storage firms can match performance at lower overhead.

00:05:25.372 --> 00:05:33.532
The first nine months of data therefore function as both a tailwind for demand and a signal that the competitive set is widening faster than before.

